{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-14319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-14319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $3.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $3-3=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{3}-3(tx)^{2}(ty)+(tx)(ty)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}+(tx)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3})-{t}^{3}(3{x}^{2}y)+{t}^{3}(x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-14319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa9eeb606c069aa5226f07b04ddcdb323200004270309fc007d683aeb98aecf5"} {"question" "q-14319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-14319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3xy+xy}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-14319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa9eeb606c069aa5226f07b04ddcdb323200004270309fc007d683aeb98aecf5"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa9eeb606c069aa5226f07b04ddcdb323200004270309fc007d683aeb98aecf5"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+xy}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-90597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^{2}+(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa9eeb606c069aa5226f07b04ddcdb323200004270309fc007d683aeb98aecf5"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{x+y}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aa9eeb606c069aa5226f07b04ddcdb323200004270309fc007d683aeb98aecf5"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcddea8b520b2772"} {"question" "q-8747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{vx}{x})} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d14fe70966ea9768012cec677886136e9f2551f40183c63dcdd