erential equation.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y^{2} - x^{2}}{xy}$"] [17592186087393 :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$"] [17592186087393 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot x}; = \\frac{y}{x}; = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0$. Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1$:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)^{2}); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t^{2} \\cdot x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot f(x, y); = t^{-1}f(x, y)\\cdot$\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= (ty)/(2(tx))\\sin (((tx)^{2})/((ty)^{2})); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot 2x}\\sin (\\frac{t^{2}x^{2}}{t^{2}y^{2}}); = \\frac{y}{2x}\\sin (\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}); = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186087391 :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$ (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\cos \\left(\\frac {y} {x} \\right)$\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x$."] [17592186087391 :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = x\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} + v &= \\cos (v) \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\cos (v) - v \\\\\n((1)/(\\cos (v) - v))\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087392 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\frac{x^2+y^2}{2xy}$."] [17592186087392 :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)^{2} + (ty)^{2})/(2(tx)(ty)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{t^{2}x^{2} + t^{2}y^{2}}{2t^{2}y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{2xy} \\\\\n&= f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n\n$$\ny=vx, \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = v + x \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} \\,\n$$\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{2xy} \\\\\nv + x\\frac{dv}{dx} &= (x^{2} + (vx)^{2})/(2x(vx)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} + v^{2}x^{2}}{2x^{2}v} \\\\\n&= (x^{2}(1 + v^{2}))/(2x^{2}v) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} - v \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} - \\frac{2v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\n\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}\\frac{dv}{dx}dx &= ∫\\frac{1}{x}dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}dv &= \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x & + C_{1} \\\\ -\\ln & 1 - v^{2}\\end{vmatrix}|= \\ln \\mid C_{2}x \\mid \\\\\n\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{1 - v^{2}} & &= \\ln & C_{2}x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - v^{2}} &= C_{2}x \\\\\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{1}{C_{2}x} \\\\\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nC_1=\\ln{C_2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{C_2}=C_3\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nand simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n1 - (\\frac{y}{x})^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n1 - \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= 1 - \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= 1 + \\frac{C_{4}}{x} \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2}(1 + \\frac{C_{4}}{x}) \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C_{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_4 = -C_3$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C_{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084694 17592186084695 17592186084696 17592186084697 17592186084698 17592186087217 17592186087218 17592186087219 17592186087220 17592186087221 17592186087223 17592186087224 17592186087225 17592186087226 17592186087227 17592186087229 17592186087230 17592186087231 17592186087232 17592186087233 17592186087259 17592186087260 17592186087261 17592186087262 17592186087263] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084693 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084693 :answer-field/id] [17592186084693 :answer-field/key] [17592186084693 :answer-field/type] [17592186087216 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087216 :answer-field/id] [17592186087216 :answer-field/key] [17592186087216 :answer-field/type] [17592186087222 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087222 :answer-field/id] [17592186087222 :answer-field/key] [17592186087222 :answer-field/type] [17592186087228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087228 :answer-field/id] [17592186087228 :answer-field/key] [17592186087228 :answer-field/type] [17592186087258 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087258 :answer-field/id] [17592186087258 :answer-field/key] [17592186087258 :answer-field/type]] "no_history"